{"authored" [{"question" "e-14720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14720.json" "file_sha256" "ffc1cea693eadebca1d0722193a9d3e73952f795fc203f350270288f864689b7" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14720.json" "file_sha256" "ffc1cea693eadebca1d0722193a9d3e73952f795fc203f350270288f864689b7" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14719.json" "file_sha256" "0cdc319106ac8db98fe7eea74bc885352729f401077493776898eaf46a879804" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14719.json" "file_sha256" "0cdc319106ac8db98fe7eea74bc885352729f401077493776898eaf46a879804" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14721.json" "file_sha256" "7498192180fc73b1bdd062e14c0278baa3f43e019b4ac0b34c49325d0038a118" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14721.json" "file_sha256" "7498192180fc73b1bdd062e14c0278baa3f43e019b4ac0b34c49325d0038a118" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14722.json" "file_sha256" "ed2f86fa3614aef04eaeed13dfadf4132c5147827078633f40125a7cb6af2d3e" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14722.json" "file_sha256" "ed2f86fa3614aef04eaeed13dfadf4132c5147827078633f40125a7cb6af2d3e" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/08ce8625dcad742776846497c4e49b5686a893be85ff1bd5426df8f8b9ce882b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on both $y=f(x)$ and $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A\\,(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $(0,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-5$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=3$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=1+2{e}^{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(3).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(3)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(3) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =3 \\\\ 1+2{e}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(3)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =1+2{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{{e}^{-4}}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{e}^{4}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14720.json" "file_sha256" "6927b7e2adc2a3da44fc320642804f51a712731526b6e6c6dbf52710b2f43ed0" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14720.json" "file_sha256" "6927b7e2adc2a3da44fc320642804f51a712731526b6e6c6dbf52710b2f43ed0" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14719.json" "file_sha256" "0cdc319106ac8db98fe7eea74bc885352729f401077493776898eaf46a879804" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14719.json" "file_sha256" "0cdc319106ac8db98fe7eea74bc885352729f401077493776898eaf46a879804" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14721.json" "file_sha256" "7498192180fc73b1bdd062e14c0278baa3f43e019b4ac0b34c49325d0038a118" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14721.json" "file_sha256" "7498192180fc73b1bdd062e14c0278baa3f43e019b4ac0b34c49325d0038a118" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14722.json" "file_sha256" "ed2f86fa3614aef04eaeed13dfadf4132c5147827078633f40125a7cb6af2d3e" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14722.json" "file_sha256" "ed2f86fa3614aef04eaeed13dfadf4132c5147827078633f40125a7cb6af2d3e" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "9a723fa5f6bed3c951eb4c36c35af58ee0427904fb2ed0595706990cb9fdf986" "basis" 1646 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2)=\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1,$ $g(2)=-3,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2) & =\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(-1)}+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "9a723fa5f6bed3c951eb4c36c35af58ee0427904fb2ed0595706990cb9fdf986" "basis" 1646 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/mode