8944/state.json" "file_sha256" "79c896f3dad333b8c0301ebe0692feab9f2d7fae4751845673201b3895343dc5" "basis" 2324 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-75986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "79c896f3dad333b8c0301ebe0692feab9f2d7fae4751845673201b3895343dc5" "basis" 2324 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-15716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/a7a711eed2b4805f0e9dfc64c0992acfef26430e37f8df446b08055143a6cadc.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,5)$ and lies on $y=f.$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(5,2)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(2)=4,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B\\,(5,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(5)=2.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(2)=4.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-15716" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15716" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15716" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $(0,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/517276d5122f1a81b894da7be7796c5941a16442987f99f476220979bc5688c7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(3,2.6)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(2.6,3)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{3},$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2.6)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B\\,(2.6,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2.6)=3.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2.6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2.6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-15719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2.6"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/08ce8625dcad742776846497c4e49b5686a893be85ff1bd5426df8f8b9ce882b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on both $y=f(x)$ and $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A\\,(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/24d83be9fed627018111aff41b976881c98c6c58b2ceeb0ff11c033f9926afd3.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(3,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $(2,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [1,5]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-15667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-15667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $P(4,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(1,4)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(4,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(4,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-342581" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342581" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-2526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-2526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-2526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-2526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-2526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-2526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-312579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ and the point $P(2,6)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-312579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,6).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,6)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(6,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-312579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-312579" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-312579" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-2294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3$ and the point $P(2,11)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(11).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-2294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,11).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =12\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,11)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(11,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(11) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(11))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-2294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-2294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-2294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-2294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-2$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-5$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(3)={g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3)+\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=-2,$ $g(3)=-3,$ ${g}^{′}(3)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=-4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & ={g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3)+\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(-2)} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{(-3)}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-110308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)$  of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n| $-3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4)={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=-3,$ $g(4)=3,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-3)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & ={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{3}{{f}^{′}(-3)} \\\\  & =-3+\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-110307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{12}{5}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "-\\frac{6}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{12}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-5$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=3$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $4$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(0)={g}^{′}(0){f}^{-1}(0)+\\frac{g(0)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(0)=-3,$ $g(0)=1,$ ${g}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{3},$ and ${f}^{′}(-3)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & ={g}^{′}(0){f}^{-1}(0)+\\frac{g(0)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-3)} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-342597" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-342597" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2)=\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1,$ $g(2)=-3,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2) & =\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(-1)}+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=1+2{e}^{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(3).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(3)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(3) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =3 \\\\ 1+2{e}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(3)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =1+2{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{e}^{-4}}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2{e}^{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={3}^{x}-1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(8).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(8)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(8) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =8 \\\\ {3}^{x}-1 & =8 \\\\ {3}^{x} & =9 \\\\ {3}^{x} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(8)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={3}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-110497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-76115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-76115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ For $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =\\sqrt{2x-1} \\\\ 1 & =2x-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(2x-1)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1-1)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-76115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-76115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-76115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-76115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-75986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-75986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=5.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4)+\\frac{x}{x-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-4)+\\frac{5}{5-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-75986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-75986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-75986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-75986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={2}^{x}-7.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(9).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(9),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(9)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(9))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(9).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(9)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(9) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =9 \\\\ {2}^{x}-7 & =9 \\\\ {2}^{x} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{x} & ={2}^{4} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(9)=4.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x}-7 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =16\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(9))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6672714dce0f8abab2c26b4ec26ed4a21efed6366014d7c5228d2c443f65979"} {"question" "q-110345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "q-110345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/state.json" "file_sha256" "56b47981fc0b7bfc3e1322919eb1cc575937d446e96c03e64b06e4a76775c6fc"} {"question" "e-14720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/example-14720.json" "file_sha256" "829124ae6ec428def9d499755942c991b60983b748e2c40af3eb55654174c0c8"} {"question" "e-14720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/example-14720.json" "file_sha256" "829124ae6ec428def9d499755942c991b60983b748e2c40af3eb55654174c0c8"} {"question" "e-14719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/example-14719.json" "file_sha256" "67f1711d76f7702aa9ab64ab561f509b12919e223ff45b849f7d6582d60c9ce4"} {"question" "e-14719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/example-14719.json" "file_sha256" "67f1711d76f7702aa9ab64ab561f509b12919e223ff45b849f7d6582d60c9ce4"} {"question" "e-14721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/example-14721.json" "file_sha256" "34e80dd56259e1bda40070672d3da206513fbd78f45bbf73754c5813ae70fec3"} {"question" "e-14721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/example-14721.json" "file_sha256" "34e80dd56259e1bda40070672d3da206513fbd78f45bbf73754c5813ae70fec3"} {"question" "e-14722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/example-14722.json" "file_sha256" "5749ad87de0b329e8138bdffb4f73cc8b0223212fa085c2f95f660daba5932b9"} {"question" "e-14722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/example-14722.json" "file_sha256" "5749ad87de0b329e8138bdffb4f73cc8b0223212fa085c2f95f660daba5932b9"}] "usage" {"q-312578" [] "e-14719" [] "q-75986" [] "q-342583" [] "q-2294" [] "q-312579" [] "e-14720" [] "q-312583" [] "e-14722" [] "q-76128" [] "q-342562" [] "q-19201" [] "e-14721" [] "q-15667" []} "basis" 2879 "retractions" [[17592186295808 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186295822 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/08ce8625dcad742776846497c4e49b5686a893be85ff1bd5426df8f8b9ce882b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on both $y=f(x)$ and $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}